 and sent it to bigquery then  on line 21 is where we're doing natural  write to bigquery and our function is  effectively done we do have a console  log statement here  I should have cleaned this up it's not  actually doing anything useful for us at  this point but the logs that I write to  the console here are actually available  in stackdriver  so if you're setting up your function  and you're like hey did I actually get  the data there or and kind of curious  what the events look like that sort of  stuff  you can do your kind of printf  console.log inspection and all that  information shows up and stackdriver  okay so jumping back to the cloud  console real quick  from here pardon me I actually skipped  stackdriver  so let's let's pull up stackdriver first  so stackdriver if if folks aren't  familiar is the kind of monitoring user  site monitoring hub for your apps and  usage of GCP on GCP so here we can see  essentially a metric graph it looks very  similar to the overview excuse me the  overview that we had back on the  function page but it does give you the  ability to do broader time spans so we  can actually look back and see the  development work that picked up in the  middle of the may for this particular  demo you can even do custom time frames  if you wanted to as well as real-time  dashboards if this is something that you  want to kind of put in your your team's  bullpen or something like that so let's  jump back down to an hour so right now  we've got three different charts so  we're tracking the number of executions  of our cloud function per second in the  case of stackdriver this is being  aggregated up to permanent buckets so we  can see over time again that little peak  when I manually change the database  scrolling down we can see the cloud  function instance counts so this is one  of the things that's really powerful  about cloud functions is it essentially  it takes care of all of the scaling  factors for you so by us triggering  events and events being in queued to be  processed GCP figures out how many  functions it needs to scale up for you  and when they can able be able to scale  down and back up and those sorts of  things so that's why you're seeing some  kind of like spike eNOS is when it's  scaling down it's trying to save me  money when it's scaling back up  giving it more work to do the final  chart down at the bottom is a pretty  cool little chart the stackdriver  provides which gives you a heat map so  instead of having to look at your like  seven lines for your p 50 and p 95 and  all that stuff it'll actually just  render a heat graph for you so we can  see here that most of our events are  processing in about 100 milliseconds  this little hot spot down here when I  did the manual trigger again but this is  nice because you can kind of start to  see things over time if we were to  expand this to the last week we can see  that it's been running relatively  consistent we've had a couple high  latency pokes at a couple different  point in times but you know nothing  super concerning as they've kind of come  back down it looks like our peaks or  about five seconds or so so jumping back  to the cloud console from here we're  going to jump into bigquery and take a  look at the data set so this is the  schema of the pixel tables you notice it  looks exactly like the JSON document  that we look like looked at in the  function unlike firestore bigquery is  schema but this is to help out with  query planning and just kind of the  dynamic nature and analytics and the  fact that it's SQL oriented so we click  on preview we can actually see a summary  of some of the data that's in there so 4  pixels 66 comma 103 we changed from red  to blue partition stay the time and then  we've got the respective time stamps  this all supports kind of your standard  SQL that you're familiar with so I could  do like select star from the cog console  Esso has nice auto completion for you  which is really great because inevitably  you end up getting kind of long names  and historical names and all that stuff  let's see order by  t-1 updated descending limit and we can  it go and it'll essentially just start  crunching so in our full database so far  we've got something like 600 million  events that have been stuck in our data  warehouse and that's in the span of  about two weeks so as as apps get more  sort of IOT and user intensive you can  imagine like just having a database that  can scale out and give you quick queries  is is really valuable and that's what  we're getting from bigquery so we're  gonna jump down here and look at a view  real quick  so the views that we've got defined here  are essentially what we're using to  drive the charts that I'll show you in  just a second you can see that they're  kind of standard SQL you know select  from table group by ordering limits all  that same normal stuff so you're not  having to learn a brand new like  charting library or anything like that  you can just write your SQL statements  and then load that up in your dashboard  so we'll jump over here to the fire  pixels dashboard so this is a dashboard  that I've created in data studio one of  the things that's really nice about data  studio dashboards is it fully integrates  with all of your G suite organization  structure so this dashboard that I'm  showing you right now is actually shared  to my demo user from my main work user  so this is really nice if you need to  like put it on a display in your bullpen  or if you only want to share it with  maybe your manager before it's published  to the rest of the company you've got  full ACL control the same way that you  do with Google Docs so here on the top  row we can see three different charts  with color breakdowns  so these color breakdowns are answering  the questions that we mentioned kind of  at the beginning of the demo around the  business questions so the business  question is essentially how many or  excuse me for the top row is what is the  ratio of a particular color per time  bucket over a period of time so here  we're seeing for the last 30 minutes  about 63% of the color changes were to  blue and the other 37 or so were to  yellow so this makes it really easy to  kind of look at the charts and having  some context around the way that the app  behaves you can just kind of look at it  and get an idea of what the app probably  looked like at that point in time and  then that expands to two days ago as  well as over the past week in the bottom  left we've got our line chart that's  showing the number of distinct colors  that were changed to in that time frame  so here we can see that we had two that  we changed to one time we only had one  one time we actually got all the way up  to three you know just various  interesting things and then the center  chart is showing our ingestion latency  so this is the essentially showing in  seconds how long it's taking from the  document being written into firestore  for us to write it into bigquery so this  is essentially showing us an end-to-end  latency of getting data into our data  warehouse so the vast majority are being  handled in less than two seconds and  we've got a little bit of a kind of  plateau here this is actually I believe  due to the fact that the cloud functions  are spinning back up after being spun  down between stages of the simulation  running and then there's almost a full  drop off by the time we reach seven  seconds so the vast vast majority of all  of our events are being put into our  data warehouse in eight seconds or so  [Music]
 

